# Suppresses /etc/xdg/autostart/org.kde.xwaylandvideobridge.desktop.
#
# home/apps.nix links this ONE FILE, by build-time-resolved store path, into
# ~/.config/autostart/ via xdg.configFile. Not the directory: that directory
# holds entries this repo does not own -- 1password, bitwarden, deskflow,
# syncthingtray and displaycal on suffer -- and linking the tree would take
# them out.
#
# Why it exists. uwsm runs XDG autostart; the hand-rolled session this repo used
# before it did not. The old hyprland.start hook started exactly three things,
# and /etc/xdg/autostart was never read. uwsm's wayland-session@ target Wants
# wayland-session-xdg-autostart@, so on the first uwsm login ten entries started
# that never had under Hyprland -- most of them wanted, this one not.
#
# xwaylandvideobridge is KDE's shim that lets X11 applications record Wayland
# screens and windows. It came in as a dependency of kwin-wayland, with Plasma,
# and nothing Hyprland asked for it. Under Plasma the shell hides its window;
# Hyprland has no reason to, so it appears as a bare XWayland window titled
# "Wayland to X Recording bridge".
#
# Why it is off rather than hidden. Nothing on this machine can use it. A
# Wayland-native application does not need the bridge -- it calls the ScreenCast
# portal directly, and xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland provides one. Checked on
# suffer: kitty and chrome are Wayland-native, the only flatpak is Slack, and
# Slack's `sockets=wayland;pulseaudio;fallback-x11` grants an X11 socket only
# when Wayland is absent -- so it runs native and shares through the portal. No
# discord, zoom, obs, teams or element, natively or as flatpaks. The bridge was
# the only XWayland client running, and it existed to serve clients that are
# not there. It cost ~36 MB by systemd's accounting for its unit.
#
# Hidden=true, not a masked unit. systemd-xdg-autostart-generator(8) documents
# "Hidden=, X-systemd-skip=: No service will be generated if set to true", and
# XDG reads ~/.config/autostart before /etc/xdg/autostart, so the copy here
# wins. Verified both ways on suffer: with this file present,
# `systemctl --user daemon-reload` leaves no
# app-org.kde.xwaylandvideobridge@autostart.service at all; remove it, reload,
# and the unit comes back. A mask would work today and break the day the
# upstream unit is renamed, and it is local state no repo replicates.
#
# To undo: remove the
# xdg.configFile."autostart/org.kde.xwaylandvideobridge.desktop" entry from
# home/apps.nix and switch again -- deleting the symlink by hand does not
# stick, the next switch's linkGeneration recreates it. Then
# `systemctl --user daemon-reload` and log back in. For a one-off, just run
# `xwaylandvideobridge` -- nothing here removes the program, only its
# autostart.
[Desktop Entry]
Type=Application
Name=Xwayland Video Bridge
Hidden=true
